  "notes": "This file layers four fundamentally different, non-interchangeable data types and none should be treated as a single authoritative 'cost of living index': (1) poverty_line_by_province is BPS's official Garis Kemiskinan (GK) — a per-capita subsistence-level monthly expenditure threshold used to define poverty, NOT a typical household budget; it is the most consistent, officially structured province-level series and is the backbone of this file. (2) cpi_by_city is BPS's Consumer Price Index / inflation data by expenditure group — this turned out to be the weakest-covered section: BPS publishes full IHK-by-group tables by city (see 'Indeks Harga Konsumen 150 Kabupaten/Kota di Indonesia 2025' publication cited above), but that table lives in a PDF/JS-rendered publication that automated web tools could not extract cell-by-cell in this session, so only partial, mostly headline/'biggest mover' figures could be retrieved for a handful of cities plus the national aggregate — this section should be treated as illustrative only, not a substitute for pulling the BPS publication directly. (3) numbeo_cost_breakdown_by_city gives absolute IDR figures (rent, food, transport, utilities) but is explicitly CROWDSOURCED/user-submitted, self-selected toward expat and urban-middle-class respondents, and can be sparse or volatile, especially outside Java/Bali. (4) khl_breakdown_by_region is Kemnaker's official 2025/2026 Kebutuhan Hidup Layak figure — the decent-living-needs benchmark wage councils use as an input to minimum-wage decisions — but despite searching, this session could not locate a publicly available province-by-province breakdown into the individual KHL components (makanan/minuman, sandang, perumahan, pendidikan, kesehatan, transportasi, rekreasi/tabungan, etc.); only the official TOTAL KHL per province could be sourced with confidence, so 'components' is null throughout that section and should not be assumed to exist elsewhere — it would need to come from the underlying regional wage-council survey reports (Dewan Pengupahan), which are scattered, non-digitized, and locality-specific. IMPORTANT DATA QUALITY CAVEAT on poverty_line_by_province: for many provinces, the food + non-food component figures extracted from source pages did NOT arithmetically sum to the stated total garis kemiskinan (the source pages present multi-year time series and the mismatch likely reflects the extraction tool picking food/non-food figures from a different period row than the total, or true urban/rural sub-splits being conflated with the provincial total). Rather than fabricate a reconciled split, rows where food+non-food did not sum to within rounding of the stated total have food_poverty_line and non_food_poverty_line set to null, with only the total (which is corroborated by the article title/headline and is the figure BPS itself publishes as the primary series) retained; this is flagged per-row via 'component_reconciliation'. Also note many databoks articles are labeled 'Desember' for a March/September BPS round — BPS's poverty line is only measured in March and September; where source text muddled these, the period given here follows the article's own explicit month/year label, which may not perfectly align with BPS's official March/September release calendar. Coverage gaps: poverty_line_by_province has no data at all for Aceh, Riau (mainland), Sumatera Selatan, and Papua Selatan despite searching (KHL totals for these ARE available). cpi_by_city and numbeo tables cover only a handful of major cities, not the ~90 BPS-monitored cities or all 38 provincial capitals. Do not treat any figure here as a live/current price — always re-verify against the cited source before using for decisions.",
